so in recent weeks the allies have come  together to try and look at ways that  they can reduce the timelines  surrounding the process there are a  number of steps involved there's  something called the accession protocol  which all allies have to sign here at  nato and the two new members would sign  that as well in order to get to that  signature we'll have to go through  something called the accession talks  once the protocol is actually signed  then every member of the alliance has to  ratify the agreement which can take some  time and you're right in the past we've  seen instances where that process has  taken upwards of a year but given the  situation on the ground in ukraine the  fact that we have a war that's ongoing  on european territory i think all allies  are in agreement that this process needs  to be expedited so ambassador just be  clear you're hoping to get this achieved  in the coming months so what by the  summer by the end of the year  ideally i think we'd like to see it done  in a couple of months that's the goal  obviously the white house is clearly  minded to approve uh this application  anyone can ask to join but of course the  members of nato have to agree it turkey  is a stumbling block isn't it it's said  it is opposed the white house said this  week it's confident consensus can be  reached can you explain to us what's  going on and why the white house is  confident of that has president biden  been in bilateral talks well first and  foremost let me say yes there has been  an ally turkey that has come forward to  express some concerns they have specific  security concerns that they've raised  with all of the allies here at nato  headquarters and we're all taking those  concerns very seriously i think the  reason you see the confidence stemming  out of the white house and in fact many  other capitals across europe and here at  nato headquarters is simply because the  strength of the alliance really rests on  our ability to continually bring forward  disagreements debates  alternative points of view and work  through those disagreements to reach  consensus everything that nato does  has to be reached through consensus you  have to have 30 allies vote on it so we  have 73 years of experience of working  through allied differences and for that  reason i too personally am quite  confident that we'll find a way through  this just be clear then you don't think  in the end turkey will be the block  i think we will see eventually sweden  and finland joining the alliance in the  not too distant future
 

